## 4. Data Sources, Licensing & Trust

| Data | Source | How it is used |
|---|---|---|
| School list, location, types, Google rating | Google Places API | Imported; rating shown as "Google 4.6 ★ (212)" |
| Drive times | Google Routes API | Computed on request; nothing stored but a daily count |
| Board, levels, admissions, facilities, achievements | The school's own website | Suggested by the reader, accepted by an admin, shown with the page it came from |
| CBSE affiliation | CBSE public record (SARAS) | Confirms a number the school states |
| Photos | The school (with permission) or Wikimedia Commons | Commons photos always show author and licence |
| Anything a school edits | The school's staff | Live, logged, undoable |
| Reviews | Parents | Moderated before publishing |

---

## 5. Privacy & Security

- The parent's location is never stored; enquiries collect no child data; reviews are anonymous.
- Row level security on every table; parents read only visible schools and their own data; writes to sensitive data go through database functions that check who is asking; signed-out visitors cannot call any of them.
- No secret keys in the app, the portal or the repositories; edge functions act as the signed-in person.
- Every school-profile change is logged and undoable.

---

## 6. Technical Architecture

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Parent app | React Native (Expo), a single `App.js` runnable in Snack (web and Expo Go); react-native-svg for the drawings |
| Partner Portal | Next.js (React) |
| Backend / database | Supabase: PostgreSQL with row level security, SQL functions and triggers, Storage (school photos), Auth |
| Server functions | Supabase Edge Functions (Deno): commute-times (Google Routes), read-school-websites (the reader), ingest-mumbai-schools |
| Integrations | Google Places API, Google Routes API (computeRouteMatrix), CBSE SARAS record, Wikimedia Commons API |

**Database changes** ship as numbered migrations (`supabase/migrations/`), run in order in the Supabase SQL Editor; each is safe to run more than once.

---

## 7. Quality

Every feature ships with automated tests:
- the database rules against an in-memory Postgres;
- the edge functions against stand-in web pages and databases;
- the app and portal screens in a simulated browser.

"Mutation testing" then breaks the code on purpose, hundreds of ways, to prove the tests notice. The rare breakages the tests don't catch are either shown to change nothing or given a new test.

